Falsifies `timetable::proofs::a_zero_period_is_never_due`.

The defect: the zero guard is deleted. This is the plausible one rather than a contrived one,
because the guard looks dead: `parse` refuses `every 0s`, so no document can reach it, and a
reader tidying the function has every reason to believe the branch is unreachable. The doc
comment on `next_after` says the guard is there for the caller who did not read the doc comment,
which is the caller a `pub const fn` has to be total for.

Expected to fail: the harness's `assert_eq!(next_after(prev, 0, now), u64::MAX)`, either on the
answer (`prev` is returned when `prev > now`) or on the remainder operation, which Kani reports
as a division by zero on the path where it is reached.

